high confidence.  Witness: |A_1^r| \cdot t = n^{1 - 2/k} \lceil 100 n^{1/k} \log n \rceil = \Omega(n^{1-1/k} \log n), \text{ which for } k = \lfloor \log_2 n \rfloor \text{ gives } |A_1^r| \cdot t \ge 50 n \log n > n \text{ for all } n \ge 2, \text{ contradicting } |A_i^r| \cdot t \le n\text{ and violating } \frac{t}{1 + |A_i^r|t/n} > \frac{t}{2}.\text{ In [p11-3], the step } \frac{t}{1 + |A^r_i|t/n} \cdot |A^r_i| > (t/2) \cdot |A^r_i| \text{ requires } |A^r_i|t < n, \text{ which is justified in [p12-17] by asserting } |A^r_i| \cdot t \le n. \text{ However, for } i=1 \text{ and } k = \Theta(\log n), |A_1^r| = n^{1-2/k} = \Theta(n) \text{ while } t = \lceil 100 n^{1/k} \log n \rceil = \Theta(\log n), \text{ so } |A_1^r| \cdot t = \Omega(n \log n) > n. \text{ Thus, } |A_i^r| \cdot t \le n \text{ does not hold, and the lower bound } \mathbb{E}[X_i] > (t/2) \cdot |A_i^r| \text{ fails to hold as stated.} 
location: Proof of Lemma 4.4 (page 12) 
